[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521569 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fbc408b-c121-5287-963f-06a800c703d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520797} {:answer/id #uuid "132bb860-1537-5d50-8e8d-eab1cc552fe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520807} {:answer/id #uuid "3ee69202-d794-5dc7-b9e1-48471ca549b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520904} {:answer/id #uuid "8d9f692f-ebf1-58e1-8b6f-d036750bb7c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521085} {:answer/id #uuid "f63824fa-c71a-5d2f-8dd7-c1adb8d01387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f63824fa-c71a-5d2f-8dd7-c1adb8d01387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521304} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d98aaaa-2215-595e-9cfe-13f69f4453bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7892f8a-0a7a-5c55-9033-6382c732bb3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-123546" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡{110}^{∘}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{18})$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡{140}^{∘}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{9})$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡{170}^{∘}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{17\\pi }{18})$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Converting ${110}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{110}^{∘}=110\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{11\\pi }{18}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Converting ${140}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{140}^{∘}=140\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{7\\pi }{9}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Converting ${170}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{170}^{∘}=170\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{17\\pi }{18}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III'."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec3cb01-9709-5f4d-b512-edefe3f3ee1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{185}{4},- \\frac{55}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{3}{2},\\,y - \\frac{5}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521677} {:answer/id #uuid "2f284a02-2fb2-5fa7-bd41-576dd3d0bba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨- \\frac{55}{2},\\frac{185}{4}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{3}{2},\\,y - \\frac{5}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521689} {:answer/id #uuid "7d40b20a-3844-5ee1-965b-acec820bd31d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨- \\frac{185}{4},- \\frac{55}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{3}{2},\\,y - \\frac{5}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521710} {:answer/id #uuid "7f241a59-debf-5de1-a40a-8220ae6d711f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{185}{4},- \\frac{55}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{3}{2},\\,y + \\frac{5}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521712} {:answer/id #uuid "8a532d49-8d53-5a2f-a45c-6713f991d1b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{185}{4},- \\frac{55}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x - \\frac{3}{2},\\,y - \\frac{5}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ec3cb01-9709-5f4d-b512-edefe3f3ee1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{185}{4},- \\frac{55}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{3}{2},\\,y - \\frac{5}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521677} :answer-field/id #uuid "b665079c-c019-5428-b8de-1f2f603c7242" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "20ccc6f9-9078-5553-ae96-b36095f293ae" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=- 5 x^{2} + 5 x y^{2} + 2 y^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(- \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(- \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- 5 x^{2} + 5 x y^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 5 x^{2} + 5 x y^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(- 10 x + 5 y^{2})\\,i+(10 x y + 4 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}) & =(- 10 (- \\frac{3}{2}) + 5 (\\frac{5}{2})^{2})\\,i+(10 (- \\frac{3}{2}) (\\frac{5}{2}) + 4 (\\frac{5}{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{185}{4})\\,i+(- \\frac{55}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{185}{4},- \\frac{55}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2})\\cdot ⟨x-(- \\frac{3}{2}),\\,y-(\\frac{5}{2})⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\frac{185}{4},- \\frac{55}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{3}{2},\\,y - \\frac{5}{2}⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521695 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21544ba6-ef06-5266-93a1-313a526067e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{85}{9},- \\frac{13}{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{5}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{3}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521685} {:answer/id #uuid "2eb23044-bcc9-5962-9105-7de29057842a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{85}{9},- \\frac{13}{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨x - \\frac{5}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{3}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521688} {:answer/id #uuid "46b191a3-b090-5dd0-ac4f-c90400eb199a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨- \\frac{13}{3},\\frac{85}{9}⟩\\cdot ⟨x - \\frac{5}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{3}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521691} {:answer/id #uuid "6c6a3182-68ef-5d3d-8b37-e9e7be34344c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨- \\frac{85}{9},- \\frac{13}{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨x - \\frac{5}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{3}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521704} {:answer/id #uuid "8b23529b-86a4-53b3-8086-beeeb9b129c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{85}{9},- \\frac{13}{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨x - \\frac{5}{2},\\,y + \\frac{1}{3}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2eb23044-bcc9-5962-9105-7de29057842a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{85}{9},- \\frac{13}{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨x - \\frac{5}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{3}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521688} :answer-field/id #uuid "854ed5c8-bf23-5931-b481-8a38951b43b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f9f3451-ede1-5210-8b07-6d5429ad7a60" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} - 5 x y^{2} + 6 y^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x^{2} - 5 x y^{2} + 6 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x^{2} - 5 x y^{2} + 6 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(4 x - 5 y^{2})\\,i+(- 10 x y + 12 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{3}) & =(4 (\\frac{5}{2}) - 5 (\\frac{1}{3})^{2})\\,i+(- 10 (\\frac{5}{2}) (\\frac{1}{3}) + 12 (\\frac{1}{3}))\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{85}{9})\\,i+(- \\frac{13}{3})\\,j \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{85}{9},- \\frac{13}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot ⟨x-(\\frac{5}{2}),\\,y-(\\frac{1}{3})⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\frac{85}{9},- \\frac{13}{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨x - \\frac{5}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{3}⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521719 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08396be8-551a-56f0-be99-5d1834a0c9d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-20,- \\frac{111}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{3}{2},\\,y + \\frac{5}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521676} {:answer/id #uuid "10e86575-f93c-5f64-950f-c7b82d9e1364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨- \\frac{111}{2},-20⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{3}{2},\\,y - \\frac{5}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521678} {:answer/id #uuid "186cfe4b-4dfa-530b-9d0d-dd50f922acde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨- \\frac{111}{2},-20⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{3}{2},\\,y + \\frac{5}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521680} {:answer/id #uuid "1d12fff6-b9f1-57fd-a747-267328625646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{111}{2},-20⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{3}{2},\\,y + \\frac{5}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521682} {:answer/id #uuid "1d6289d5-24ff-5c6c-97cc-fd398c028f1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨- \\frac{111}{2},-20⟩\\cdot ⟨x - \\frac{3}{2},\\,y + \\frac{5}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "186cfe4b-4dfa-530b-9d0d-dd50f922acde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨- \\frac{111}{2},-20⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{3}{2},\\,y + \\frac{5}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521680} :answer-field/id #uuid "92ce6bee-2817-5c2a-8469-519cf78c9bd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8eb72b3b-1817-5b03-8db4-35e0f0a341f5" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=6 x^{2} - 6 x y^{2} - 5 y^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{5}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{5}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6 x^{2} - 6 x y^{2} - 5 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6 x^{2} - 6 x y^{2} - 5 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(12 x - 6 y^{2})\\,i+(- 12 x y - 10 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{5}{2}) & =(12 (- \\frac{3}{2}) - 6 (- \\frac{5}{2})^{2})\\,i+(- 12 (- \\frac{3}{2}) (- \\frac{5}{2}) - 10 (- \\frac{5}{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =(- \\frac{111}{2})\\,i+(-20)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨- \\frac{111}{2},-20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{5}{2})\\cdot ⟨x-(- \\frac{3}{2}),\\,y-(- \\frac{5}{2})⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨- \\frac{111}{2},-20⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{3}{2},\\,y + \\frac{5}{2}⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521722 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bd85355-271e-50bb-88b1-d2b13a06d756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨- \\frac{119}{4},\\frac{3}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{5}{2},\\,y + \\frac{1}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521693} {:answer/id #uuid "5706501c-de86-5634-87a7-ddcfe0773c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨- \\frac{119}{4},\\frac{3}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{5}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521697} {:answer/id #uuid "66a910cc-c44b-5f7c-b648-cb1fe9aff95d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨- \\frac{119}{4},\\frac{3}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x - \\frac{5}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521701} {:answer/id #uuid "672bcb75-b4ca-5b71-9acb-7ec472d66c38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{119}{4}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{5}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521702} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3912a8-6f0e-56f9-a5f1-d09dbac9861c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{119}{4},\\frac{3}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{5}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5706501c-de86-5634-87a7-ddcfe0773c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨- \\frac{119}{4},\\frac{3}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{5}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521697} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e6fa0b3-7471-5b24-b6ed-74973563e2be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "973a3ce1-53a3-5558-8746-a27a1f5bd217" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=6 x^{2} + x y^{2} + 4 y^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(- \\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(- \\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6 x^{2} + x y^{2} + 4 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6 x^{2} + x y^{2} + 4 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(12 x + y^{2})\\,i+(2 x y + 8 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{2}) & =(12 (- \\frac{5}{2}) + (\\frac{1}{2})^{2})\\,i+(2 (- \\frac{5}{2}) (\\frac{1}{2}) + 8 (\\frac{1}{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =(- \\frac{119}{4})\\,i+(\\frac{3}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =⟨- \\frac{119}{4},\\frac{3}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot ⟨x-(- \\frac{5}{2}),\\,y-(\\frac{1}{2})⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨- \\frac{119}{4},\\frac{3}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{5}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d22a663-48a2-55aa-87ea-92afafe41064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨- \\frac{3}{2},3⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{1}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521699} {:answer/id #uuid "67e3d0eb-1a49-5657-bd18-9920d2fdd192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,- \\frac{3}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{1}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521703} {:answer/id #uuid "7e070eab-5072-5578-8ddd-95b7ea361e77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨- \\frac{3}{2},3⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{1}{2},\\,y + \\frac{1}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521711} {:answer/id #uuid "84fdbbbf-e7b5-59c4-baca-28b3b77d3ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨- \\frac{3}{2},3⟩\\cdot ⟨x - \\frac{1}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521714} {:answer/id #uuid "e5326f5b-b542-524b-ac5a-a375a939b9ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{2},3⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{1}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d22a663-48a2-55aa-87ea-92afafe41064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨- \\frac{3}{2},3⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{1}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521699} :answer-field/id #uuid "e884b373-05ab-556b-be57-d21657a01fc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d655f08e-ae0f-5e47-86c7-e30d50fc3764" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=3 x^{2} + 6 x y^{2} + 6 y^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3 x^{2} + 6 x y^{2} + 6 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3 x^{2} + 6 x y^{2} + 6 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(6 x + 6 y^{2})\\,i+(12 x y + 12 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}) & =(6 (- \\frac{1}{2}) + 6 (\\frac{1}{2})^{2})\\,i+(12 (- \\frac{1}{2}) (\\frac{1}{2}) + 12 (\\frac{1}{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =(- \\frac{3}{2})\\,i+(3)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨- \\frac{3}{2},3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot ⟨x-(- \\frac{1}{2}),\\,y-(\\frac{1}{2})⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨- \\frac{3}{2},3⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{1}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522702 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89474594-05e8-543d-b7ea-9e4daa46e36d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "140" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89474594-05e8-543d-b7ea-9e4daa46e36d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "140" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522295} :answer-field/id #uuid "12874409-02a6-5ec7-bc52-1cd515da4b29" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "13a313ba-f3f4-5888-a2ed-231c29e8a4fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-343310" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4723361163d4ca372019a91a347d2cb3d0aeee0ece1ace62762348dede9cea8.png)\n\nA worker is coating a surface with epoxy using a thickness gauge, which fixes the epoxy height at $5\\,\\text{in}.$ If the length is increasing at a rate of $3\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s}$ and the width is increasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s},$ how fast is the surface area of the epoxy layer increasing when its length is $12\\,\\text{in}$ and its width is $7\\,\\text{in}?$\n\nThe surface area is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $S$ be its surface area. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =12\\,\\text{in}, \\\\ y & =7\\,\\text{in}, \\\\ z & =5\\,\\text{in}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x$ and $y.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nSince $z=5\\,\\text{in}$ is not changing, we get\n\n$$\nS=10x+2xy+10y.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10x+2xy+10y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(10+2x)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+(10+2y)(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(10+2\\cdot 12)(2)+(10+2\\cdot 7)(3) \\\\  & =(34)(2)+(24)(3) \\\\  & =68+72 \\\\  & =140\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of the rectangular prism is increasing at a rate of $140\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522703 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f7bc959-ffc0-515f-8e2d-91ce2f38383f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f7bc959-ffc0-515f-8e2d-91ce2f38383f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522348} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3b46e6b-bbfb-526b-9cc5-0bb7578fda4b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13eab2a4-15b8-5aae-888d-a5c1961fc009" :question/math-academy-id "q-343334" :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac1e157b59bb632068e45776cdeb6bdc2dee42fd7f9b92ee2ea61401c1087d29.png)\n\nA rectangular block of ice is melting in a narrow channel that keeps its length fixed at $12\\,\\text{in}.$ Its width is decreasing by $1\\,\\text{in}\\text{/h},$ while its height is decreasing by $0.4\\,\\text{in}\\text{/h}.$ How fast is its volume decreasing when its dimensions are $5\\,\\text{in}$ in width and $4\\,\\text{in}$ in height? Assume that the volume's rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe volume is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$" :question/worked-solution "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L,$ $W,$ and $H$ be the length, width, and height of the ice block, respectively, and\n\n- let $V$ be the volume of the ice block.\n\nNote that $H,$ $W,$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ is constant. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=12\\text{ in},\\, & W=5\\text{ in}, \\\\ H=4\\text{ in},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=-1\\text{ in/h}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-0.4\\text{ in/h}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the three time-dependent variables, $V,$ $W$ and $H,$ is given by the formula for the volume of ice block:\n\n$$\nV=LWH\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWH$ with respect to $t,$ treating $L$ as a constant, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =L\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(WH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =L[W(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t})+H(\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t})] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =L\\cdot W(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(12)(5)(-0.4)+(12)(4)(-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-24+(-48) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of ice is decreasing at a rate of $72\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/h}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522715 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bcf9157-1f36-52e9-a61e-f8e687fb3456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8.5\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522118} {:answer/id #uuid "4be5baa3-e1d0-576d-8ab9-af6042eedd71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7.25\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522182} {:answer/id #uuid "9dde3e4d-67fd-59dc-b0bb-4064cd68c048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9.5\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522343} {:answer/id #uuid "a51d42a0-8eab-5b68-be60-237b93b5dc6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522368} {:answer/id #uuid "fce26c00-7a81-52cc-9e1d-4d762eae2284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522552}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4be5baa3-e1d0-576d-8ab9-af6042eedd71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7.25\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522182} :answer-field/id #uuid "e24d6138-77a7-51ac-903f-75f16a69daee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a0ddd70-eefe-5886-baf8-3017327746b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-30027" :question/problem "![](images/03/03e31c64b26e304e720ed0178ff23af8b2ca0955c678bd695e930e0a5cb9069a.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing by $0.5\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}$ and its height increasing at a rate of $1\\text{ feet/min}.$ If the volume of the rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $10{\\text{ feet}}^{3}\\text{/min},$  how fast is its width changing when its dimensions are $2\\text{ feet}$ in length, $3\\text{ feet}$ in width and $1\\text{ feet}$ in height?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\text{ feet}, \\\\ y & =1\\text{ feet}, \\\\ z & =3\\text{ feet}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.5\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =1\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-10{\\text{ feet}}^{3}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=2\\,\\text{feet},$ $y=1\\,\\text{feet},$ and $z=3\\,\\text{feet}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x\\cdot y\\cdot z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =xy(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+xz(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+yz(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =(2)(1)(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+(2)(3)(1)+(1)(3)(-0.5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-7.25\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width of the solid is decreasing at a rate of $7.25\\,\\text{feet}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=2\\,\\text{feet},$ $y=1\\,\\text{feet},$ and $z=3\\,\\text{feet}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d285a60-7326-5698-83d2-37558960d4c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d285a60-7326-5698-83d2-37558960d4c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522264} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e3e231d-940f-56e2-b89e-9c44df37d8f2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ad65545-bf82-508f-8a9d-4bdd8ed0f623" :question/math-academy-id "q-343323" :question/problem "![](images/2b/2b0876986e02941b588bf9bacbc5a0caa61eb2b95021fa4ed52408dd4f5d3f72.png)\n\nA rectangular block of clay is being reshaped as it moves through a press. Its height is decreasing at a rate of $0.5\\,\\text{ft/min},$ its width is increasing at a rate of $1\\,\\text{ ft/min},$ and its length is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{ft/min}.$ How fast is its volume changing when its dimensions are $10\\,\\text{ft}$ in length, $4\\,\\text{ft}$ in width, and $3\\,\\text{ft}$ in height? Assume that the rate of decrease of volume is positive.\n\nThe volume of the rectangular block of clay is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{ft}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $L,$ $W,$ $H$ and $V$ be the length, the width, the height, and the volume of the box, respectively. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=10\\text{ ft},\\, & W=4\\text{ ft}, \\\\ H=3\\text{ ft},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\text{ ft/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=1\\text{ ft/min},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-0.5\\text{ ft/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the four variables is given by the formula for the volume of a rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=LWH\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWH$ with respect to $t,$ using the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =W\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot W(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(4)(3)(-2)+(10)(3)(1)+(10)(4)(-0.5) \\\\  & =-14{\\text{ ft}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the rectangular block of clay is decreasing at a rate of $14{\\text{ ft}}^{3}/\\text{min}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a5b5a30-e0e7-5744-b422-7047faa2e338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522040} {:answer/id #uuid "619e746c-8ca9-559a-ba02-5e619348d205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522222} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec0dca9-31ae-5610-aaf5-c02dc410d7fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522269} {:answer/id #uuid "962be32f-d335-5bd3-b444-dcb1f56ce30c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522327} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c39bb2-9093-5603-adeb-9895d5238cf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "619e746c-8ca9-559a-ba02-5e619348d205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522222} :answer-field/id #uuid "789b2306-2fd7-57dc-bf7b-7225dc82a697" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3e0ff1de-8233-57fb-9fb2-58728b6e261f" :question/math-academy-id "q-112465" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{2}-25?$\n\nI. $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(2x-5)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(2x-5)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(ax-b)$ is a factor of the polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=\\frac{b}{a}$ is also root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(2x-5).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{5}{2}) & =4{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-25 \\\\  & =4(\\frac{25}{4})-25 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the factor theorem, since $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then $(2x-5)$ is a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $(2x-5)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ then $(2x-5)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522721 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "040a210b-491b-5e87-810f-cce446965078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522024} {:answer/id #uuid "7c38600e-9095-5d68-9679-992de9782bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522263} {:answer/id #uuid "b7f950d3-5f98-5aa6-9a5c-854d5d739f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522397} {:answer/id #uuid "d2352f86-cbc5-5a20-9312-01f56f5d1500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522456} {:answer/id #uuid "dcf9c1bf-6252-5332-8106-48f580492575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2352f86-cbc5-5a20-9312-01f56f5d1500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522456} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a3c0e7e-447d-50a1-976e-443b533f1367" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ea37247-24cf-5fab-829f-0e5fcfa54afc" :question/math-academy-id "q-66855" :question/problem "$2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}$ as a power of $e$ (the base of the natural logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}={e}^{-4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}})=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-4})\n$$\n\nThe natural logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=-4.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-4})=2\\cdot (-4)=-8\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522735 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13be8914-3939-5f35-94de-964f577dbb90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522060} {:answer/id #uuid "409a924e-c3d5-573d-a816-4ab2ade05c96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{\\pi }}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522157} {:answer/id #uuid "7f851c91-dd31-529b-8d7c-0e3c93a3423b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522272} {:answer/id #uuid "99ce6949-6298-5efa-9a63-94c02eabace3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522334} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b430b7-55aa-5295-bee6-f4c3c788ec83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13be8914-3939-5f35-94de-964f577dbb90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522060} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b20a77e-96f0-53df-9532-08e282712028" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b08de07-14d5-570b-89f3-5d4423c1c633" :question/math-academy-id "q-107854" :question/problem "Evaluate $Γ(\\frac{3}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the gamma function at multiples of one-half, we use the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\nΓ(z+1)=zΓ(z),\\,Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\sqrt{\\pi }\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ(\\frac{3}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}Γ(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522743 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26ce561d-4554-50ea-b082-6ff5e35c97e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522106} {:answer/id #uuid "6c74f04b-bcfa-5467-9414-e11ffa53e63f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522239} {:answer/id #uuid "90ecd540-4787-5529-b79c-562d35ce6e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522315} {:answer/id #uuid "ed69a781-7c92-5ec0-b5f9-cb723c492b54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522526} {:answer/id #uuid "eedee9f0-a7c7-5bdf-8d5b-a60ae7bd02f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed69a781-7c92-5ec0-b5f9-cb723c492b54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522526} :answer-field/id #uuid "8155f6ea-7ad1-5d0c-9428-1db9bd0cca29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7c0a4827-45c4-5e42-a7d2-ab4337115042" :question/math-academy-id "q-116486" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f87cf02aac4cee2680b2045b13fc2e7ce006c39c37fa470a7803c56624d6acd.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $4\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ the width is increasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ and the $6\\,\\text{cm}$ height does not change. How fast is the surface area of the rectangular prism decreasing when its dimensions are $4\\,\\text{cm}$ in length and $11\\,\\text{cm}$ in width?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $S$ be its surface area. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ y & =11\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ z & =6\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x$ and $y.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nSince $z=6\\,\\text{cm}$ is not changing, we get\n\n$$\nS=12x+2xy+12y.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12x+2xy+12y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2x+12)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+(12+2y)(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2\\cdot 4+12)(5)+(12+2\\cdot 11)(-4) \\\\  & =100-136 \\\\  & =-36\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of the rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $36\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522748 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad11d165-ba21-5fa4-bbce-11ab5622f378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "624" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad11d165-ba21-5fa4-bbce-11ab5622f378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "624" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522380} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb6a5f66-96e9-5ca5-964a-29cec265540a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84586114-96d4-5e09-89f7-bf9841d73d0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-314521" :question/problem "![](images/8c/8c4a2802cbd06159b0b0dcbee59f5b4a6e93b4c4e029dc88d04352c50471659e.png)\n\nAn elastic rectangular prism whose height is $15\\text{ in}$ and width is $24\\text{ in}$ is being stretched. If its length is increasing at a rate of $8\\text{ in/min},$ what is the rate of change of the surface area of the rectangular prism?\n\nThe surface area is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the rectangular prism, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. The variables $x,S,$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =15\\text{ in}, \\\\ z & =24\\text{ in}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =8\\text{ in}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\nS=2(x)(24)+2x(15)+2(15)(24)=78x+720.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =78(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the above expression using $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=8,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =78(8)=624.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rectangular prism's surface area is increasing at a rate of $624\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522753 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f1a2079-1fc1-5955-bbfb-db810024db1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{u}\\frac{{e}^{w}}{uv}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522088} {:answer/id #uuid "7e097b3d-1787-51eb-8d0e-f6ddc058f730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{u}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{e}^{w}}{v}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522267} {:answer/id #uuid "9096297c-682f-58dc-8315-adc51907dab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{v}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{e}^{w}}{v}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522314} {:answer/id #uuid "942264b3-d374-50be-87f0-6ee36149aa06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{e}^{w}}{uv}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522323} {:answer/id #uuid "b31ba118-b4ec-54ec-bb50-628c0cc67dda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}uv{e}^{w}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "942264b3-d374-50be-87f0-6ee36149aa06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{e}^{w}}{uv}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522323} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd889248-045c-5336-9e14-01871b41eb76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a7a1582-d778-5af4-af63-1b6090d14c6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-39062" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\frac{1}{x}\\le y\\le \\frac{2}{x},\\,\\frac{1}{xy}\\le z\\le \\frac{2}{xy}\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=xy,\\,w=xyz,\n$$\n\nthe triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\,{e}^{xyz}\\,dV\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$ \n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=xy,\\,w=xyz\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,1\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ yz & xz & xy\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =xy|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ y & x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =xy(x-0) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}y \\\\  & =uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{uv}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{e}^{xyz}\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{e}^{w}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{uv}|\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{{e}^{w}}{uv}\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{e}^{w}}{uv}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522769 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "050d72d8-9732-5d66-9749-bb6219b382a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522027} {:answer/id #uuid "64f70bec-dfba-5908-ae62-b36e526bc693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522228} {:answer/id #uuid "a4da2019-88c0-57c4-bb97-d5aa6d31b1a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522365} {:answer/id #uuid "bd9d8a80-77df-59fa-9951-2e78efff78f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522411} {:answer/id #uuid "f7779c3f-37b2-5f1d-848b-d22bcbee42f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7779c3f-37b2-5f1d-848b-d22bcbee42f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522546} :answer-field/id #uuid "26b21c09-8fe6-5a0f-b6ac-aa6ac324ee5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b031bd51-cdd6-576b-8767-0d0f9a3a9494" :question/math-academy-id "q-107883" :question/problem "$\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,Γ(\\frac{7}{2})+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,Γ(\\frac{3}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the gamma function at multiples of one-half, we use the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\nΓ(z+1)=zΓ(z),\\,Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\sqrt{\\pi }\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\nΓ(\\frac{3}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi },\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ(\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,Γ(\\frac{5}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\,Γ(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{8}\\sqrt{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,Γ(\\frac{7}{2})+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,Γ(\\frac{3}{2})=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\cdot \\frac{15}{8}\\sqrt{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi }=8.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522770 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e5a6f2-9272-5161-919b-04845a8c04cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522016} {:answer/id #uuid "227a5d12-1270-57e4-a81e-b7bae3293ff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522096} {:answer/id #uuid "6939f3f9-14d3-5391-9ce7-fd73d9fe6ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522233} {:answer/id #uuid "f49a3493-cb55-5f67-9873-fc200a3a90df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00e5a6f2-9272-5161-919b-04845a8c04cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522016} :answer-field/id #uuid "29458fa7-bc42-566e-b9be-960433c46a54" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select