5} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6fae7dc-2035-5c36-a249-17ced611b26c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cc496cc2-5b68-5fb6-ab6a-0e20b6873203" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})-10,\\,y=1\\sin(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})+0,\\,\\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\le t<\\frac{7\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-4\\sin(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n2\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{11\\pi}{6}, \\frac{7\\pi}{3}, \\frac{17\\pi}{6}, \\frac{10\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{11\\pi}{6}) & =4\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{7\\pi}{3}) & =-4\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{17\\pi}{6}) & =4\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{10\\pi}{3}) & =-4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{11\\pi}{6}, \\frac{7\\pi}{3}, \\frac{17\\pi}{6}, \\frac{10\\pi}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d66d1357-e0d7-58c9-a48f-bd62562836f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d66d1357-e0d7-58c9-a48f-bd62562836f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/ma